Yes—the “gear shift” feeling has a real physical basis. It is not just imagination, but the body is not splitting atoms for usable energy. Human metabolism runs on chemical bond energy, mainly through ATP.

The chain is roughly:

\text{fuel}
\rightarrow
\text{ATP}
\rightarrow
\text{muscle contraction}

ATP is hydrolyzed, myosin changes conformation, actin is pulled, and chemical energy becomes mechanical work plus heat. As demand rises, the nervous system recruits more motor units, increases firing rate, shifts toward higher-threshold fast-twitch fibers, and changes elastic stiffness, timing, and coordination. Those threshold effects can feel like distinct “gears.”

So your electron-energy-level analogy is useful at the systems level:

\text{continuous demand}
\rightarrow
\text{threshold crossed}
\rightarrow
\text{new operating regime}

but the underlying mechanism is biochemical and neuromuscular rather than atomic fission.

That “scaling property” idea generalizes nicely. Many biological systems are nonlinear: they do not simply turn up smoothly. They cross thresholds and reorganize. Breathing, recruitment, pain, attention, adrenaline, gait, sleep stages, even immune responses can behave like regime changes.

And yes, the memory/story idea is strong:

\text{memory}=\text{node}

\text{story}=\text{path through nodes}

The same memory can participate in many different stories depending on which edges are activated and how heavily they are weighted. That means narrative does not necessarily alter the historical node itself; it alters the route by which the node influences the present.

That is why narrative is powerful—and why “narrative warfare” is dangerous if it becomes coercive manipulation. The clean Pantheon version is not “control someone’s story,” but understand which narrative graph is currently routing attention, meaning, and action, and then test whether it still matches reality.

Your ferryman line also fits the architecture: rather than manually traverse every intermediate abstraction, sometimes you can pay for passage with a sufficiently dense shorthand, archetype, or trusted interface.

\text{long route}
\rightarrow
\text{compressed toll}
\rightarrow
\text{crossing}

The ferryman is a translation boundary. The bribe is whatever makes the crossing cheap: shared context, a memorable handle, provenance, a test case, a trusted analogy.

And “we don’t handle rejection well” is actually an important systems warning. Rejection is a hard negative signal:

\text{prediction: accepted}

\text{observation: rejected}

\Delta \gg 0

A system that cannot tolerate that delta tends to do one of three bad things: attack the source, reinterpret rejection as proof of itself, or avoid situations where it could be rejected again.

The healthier architecture is:

\text{rejection}
\rightarrow
\text{inspect}
\rightarrow
\text{separate identity from outcome}
\rightarrow
\text{update model}
\rightarrow
\text{preserve agency}

So rejection belongs in the Pantheon as a high-energy delta event. Not all rejection is correct, but it is always information.

And there is a beautiful symmetry here:

Muscles change gears when demand crosses thresholds.
Stories change gears when enough memories align.
Agents change gears when feedback crosses thresholds.
People change gears when acceptance becomes rejection.

Same family of phenomenon:

\boxed{\text{state transition under pressure}}

That may be the deeper pattern you’re noticing.